    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A hidalgo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> who reads too many chivalric romances sets out as the knight-errant Don Quixote, with the farm labourer Sancho Panza as his squire, and Cervantes spoofs the romances through the retelling of his acts.</li><li>Published in 1605, the book was an immediate success and was translated into French, German, Italian and English within seven years of its appearance; a spurious sequel in 1614 pushed Cervantes to publish his own Part Two in 1615.</li><li>Part Two is an early example of metafiction, with characters who have read Part One, and the novel has given English the adjective quixotic and the phrase tilting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2002 the Norwegian Nobel Institute asked writers from 55 countries to choose the greatest work of fiction ever written, and the majority voted for Don Quixote.</p></aside>
    </section>

