    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1604</span><span class="rf-text">Cervantes sells the rights to Don Quixote, Part One, to the publisher-bookseller Francisco de Robles for an unknown sum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1605</span><span class="rf-text">Part One appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, and by August there are two Madrid editions, two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> editions and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1612</span><span class="rf-text">Thomas Shelton's English translation of Part One appears while Cervantes is still alive</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1614</span><span class="rf-text">A spurious second part by an author using the name Avellaneda is published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1615</span><span class="rf-text">Cervantes publishes his own Part Two, finished as a direct result of the Avellaneda book</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1617</span><span class="rf-text">Parts One and Two are published together as one edition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1885</span><span class="rf-text">John Ormsby's translation appears and becomes the model for most modern English translators</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/apuleius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apuleius.jpg" alt="Apuleius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_philosopher_(probably_Apuleius_or_Lactantius)_on_late_antique_ceiling_painting_from_Trier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cervantes wrote at a time when, as the novel is said to reflect, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s standing as a world power was declining and its national treasury was bankrupt because of expensive foreign wars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> had put the Spanish Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> on the defensive, which had led to the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>, and the hidalgo class was losing relevance as changes in society made the high ideals of chivalry obsolete.</p><p>The book draws on earlier literature. Its sources include the Castilian novel Amadis de Gaula, which had been very popular throughout the 16th century, and Tirant lo Blanch, which the priest in the novel calls the best book in the world, though in what sense it was best is much debated among scholars. Cervantes also refers to the Italian poem Orlando furioso, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-golden-ass">The Golden Ass</a> appears to have been another important source. His experiences as a galley slave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> also influenced the work, as did his family's ties to the medical community. Tradition in Argamasilla de Alba holds that he was imprisoned in the cave of Medrano and began the book there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alonso Quixano is an hidalgo nearing 50 who lives in an unspecified region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> with his niece and housekeeper. His obsession with chivalric romances leads him to don an old suit of armour, rename himself Don Quixote, name his workhorse Rocinante and make Aldonza Lorenzo, whom he renames Dulcinea del Toboso, his lady. He takes an inn for a castle and is dubbed a knight by the innkeeper in a pretend ceremony. He then recruits his neighbour, the poor farm labourer Sancho Panza, as squire, with the promise of a petty governorship, and attacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> that he takes for giants.</p><p>In Part One he imposes his imagination on the world, while Sancho's spirit ascends from reality to illusion, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> de Madariaga put it. The book also contains tales told by other characters, the best known being The Ill-Advised Curiosity, about a Florentine nobleman who has a friend try to seduce his wife. In Part Two a duke and duchess who have read Part One stage practical jokes on the pair, Sancho gets a false governorship, and Don Quixote is defeated by the Knight of the White Moon and ordered to give up chivalry for a year. He then falls ill, regains his sanity as Alonso Quixano, renounces his ambition and dies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega.jpg" alt="Lope de Vega" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LopedeVega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Eugenio Caxés, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For Cervantes and the readers of his day, Don Quixote was a one-volume book published in 1605, divided internally into four parts. The mention of further adventures at its close was conventional and was not taken seriously as a plan for a continuation. In July 1604 Cervantes sold the rights to the publisher-bookseller Francisco de Robles; the licence to publish was granted in September, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> was finished in December, and the book came out on 16 January 1605. The sale deprived him of further profit on Part One.</p><p>In about September 1614 a spurious Part Two appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a> under the name of Alonso Fernández de Avellaneda, by an unidentified Aragonese who admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a>. Its prologue insulted Cervantes, who took offence. He had probably not got much further than Chapter 59 by late July 1614. His own Part Two, published in 1615, takes up the matter: Don Quixote finds Avellaneda's book being printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and meets one of its characters, Don Alvaro Tarfe, whom he makes swear that the other Quixote and Sancho are impostors. Avellaneda's identity has been the subject of many theories, with no consensus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-twain.jpg" alt="Mark Twain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_Twain_by_AF_Bradley_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.F. Bradley, New York, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The novel was an immediate success. By August 1605 there were two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> editions, two published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and pirated editions were soon being prepared. Most of the 400 copies of the first edition were sent to the New World, and although most disappeared in a shipwreck near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, about 70 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and were sent on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. Seven years after Part One appeared, it had been translated into French, German, Italian and English.</p><p>Readers have taken the book in different ways. Harold Bloom calls it the first modern novel and says its recurring theme is the human need to withstand suffering. Edith Grossman describes first reading it as the most tragic book in the world and later laughing aloud while translating it, since Cervantes never lets the reader rest. Some critics regard Part Two as better than Part One for its depth of characterisation and its philosophical insights. The book has also been said to have smiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s chivalry away. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> said it swept the world's admiration for mediaeval chivalry out of existence. In 1632 Cardinal Zapata ordered one sentence, on works of charity done in a lukewarm way, removed from future editions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book has survived through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, and its history is one of editions. After the early printings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, a third Madrid edition appeared in 1608, a seventh publication in all, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> bookseller issued an Italian edition in 1610. Parts One and Two were first published as one edition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1617. The novel is believed to have sold more than 500 million copies worldwide, and the Cervantes Collection at the State Library of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, gathered by Ben Haneman over thirty years, includes over 1,100 editions.</p><p>English translation has its own history. Thomas Shelton's version of Part One appeared in 1612 and of Part Two in 1620, though John Ormsby and Samuel Putnam found it far from satisfactory. Pierre Antoine Motteux's version of around 1700 was reprinted until recent times, yet Ormsby called it worse than worthless. The Jervas translation of 1742, printed as Jarvis through an error, was the most frequently reprinted until about 1885, and Tobias Smollett's of 1755 is still reprinted. Most modern translators take John Ormsby's of 1885 as their model, and Daniel Eisenberg, reviewing 27 of 30 English translations in 2008, found no single ideal one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas.jpg" alt="Alexandre Dumas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadar_-_Alexander_Dumas_père_(1802-1870)_-_Google_Art_Project_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book had a major influence on the literary community and is directly referenced in 19th-century French works such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>'s The Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musketeers</a> and Edmond Rostand's Cyrano de Bergerac. Its pair of characters, the tall, thin, idealistic Quixote and the fat, world-weary Panza, became a motif echoed ever since, and Sancho Panza and Rocinante are emblems of Western literary culture. It has inspired works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a> and Richard Strauss, and the French opera Don Quichotte.</p><p>The language took in its terms. The word quixotic, defined by Merriam-Webster as the foolishly impractical pursuit of ideals, was quickly adopted by many languages, and tilting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a>, meaning attacking imaginary enemies, comes from the scene of the windmills. The phrase the pot calling the kettle black also has its own article. The opening sentence created a classic Spanish cliche with de cuyo nombre no quiero acordarme. Species have been named after the novel and its characters, among them the amphipod Dulcibella camanchaca and the dinosaurs Lohuecotitan pandafilandi and Qunkasaura pintiquiniestra.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Don Quixote is Cervantes's Spanish novel of a gentleman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> who, after reading too many chivalric romances, sets out as a knight-errant with Sancho Panza. Published in 1605 and 1615, it is regarded as the first modern novel and one of the most-translated books in the world.</em></p>

